  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase Effects of Time Shifting",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine how a time shift leaves the Fourier-transform magnitude unchanged while adding the linear phase term -$\\omega$ t0 to the spectrum.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase Effects of Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Magnitude and Phase Effects of Time Shifting",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Suppose $x(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and let $Y(j\\omega)$ be the transform of the delayed signal $y(t)=x(t-t_0)$ with $t_0>0$. The cue in this step is the extra factor from the shift: separate what it does to magnitude and what it does to phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-t_0)\\leftrightarrow e^{-j\\omega t_0}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $e^{-j\\omega t_0}$ has unit magnitude for every $\\omega$, so the magnitude spectrum does not change at all. The delay only rotates the spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|Y(j\\omega)|=|X(j\\omega)|,\\qquad \\angle Y(j\\omega)=\\angle X(j\\omega)-\\omega t_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the phase gains the linear term $-\\omega t_0$, which means the phase slope changes by $-t_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If the shift is an advance instead of a delay, just flip the sign of $t_0$; the magnitude still stays unchanged, and the phase term becomes $+\\omega t_0$. Later examples will reuse exactly this pattern by keeping the magnitude fixed and reading the sign from the phase slope.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase Effects of Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Magnitude Spectrum of a Time-Shifted Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and $y(t)=x(t-2)$, what is $|Y(j\\omega)|$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the delay by $2$ in $y(t)=x(t-2)$. Use the time-shift property with the known pair $x(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, so the shifted spectrum is the original spectrum multiplied by $e^{-j2\\omega}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=e^{-j2\\omega}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now take the magnitude of both sides. The factor $e^{-j2\\omega}$ has unit magnitude for every $\\omega$, so it does not change the size of the spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|Y(j\\omega)|=|e^{-j2\\omega}|\\,|X(j\\omega)|=|X(j\\omega)|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude spectrum is unchanged by the delay, and the answer is $|Y(j\\omega)|=|X(j\\omega)|$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase Effects of Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Phase Change from a Time Delay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and $\\angle X(j\\omega)=\\omega/2$, what is $\\angle Y(j\\omega)$ for $y(t)=x(t-3)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the delay by $3$ in $y(t)=x(t-3)$. Keep the original phase $\\angle X(j\\omega)=\\omega/2$ and use the time-shift rule to add only the delay-induced linear term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=e^{-j3\\omega}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $e^{-j3\\omega}$ contributes phase $-3\\omega$, and it has unit magnitude, so it does not change the magnitude spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle Y(j\\omega)=\\angle X(j\\omega)-3\\omega=\\omega/2-3\\omega=-5\\omega/2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the new phase spectrum is $-5\\omega/2$, and the magnitude stays unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase Effects of Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Phase Change from a Time Advance",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and $\\angle X(j\\omega)=\\omega/2$, what is $\\angle Y(j\\omega)$ for $y(t)=x(t+3)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A time advance is the mirror image of the delay case. Keep the original phase $\\omega/2$ and add the advance term that comes from shifting $x(t)$ to $x(t+3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=e^{j3\\omega}X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $e^{j3\\omega}$ has unit magnitude, so the magnitude spectrum stays $|Y(j\\omega)|=|X(j\\omega)|$. Its phase contribution is $+3\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle Y(j\\omega)=\\angle X(j\\omega)+3\\omega=\\omega/2+3\\omega=7\\omega/2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the advance leaves the magnitude unchanged and shifts the phase line upward by $3\\omega$, giving $\\angle Y(j\\omega)=7\\omega/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude and Phase Effects of Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying a Time Shift from Linear Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)e^{-j4\\omega}$, what time shift does $y(t)$ represent relative to $x(t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the linear phase term first. The factor multiplying $X(j\\omega)$ is $e^{-j4\\omega}$, so compare its exponent to the standard shift form $e^{-j\\omega t_0}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-4\\omega=-\\omega t_0\\;\\Rightarrow\\;t_0=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $t_0$ is positive, the signal is delayed by 4 units: $y(t)=x(t-4)$. The factor $e^{-j4\\omega}$ has unit magnitude, so the magnitude spectrum does not change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t-4),\\qquad |Y(j\\omega)|=|X(j\\omega)|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the negative linear phase slope identifies a delay of 4, and the magnitude remains unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
